#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115370 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b58de617-785d-5318-9d12-99651e030eb2" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Using Direct Substitution"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115371 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae016162-6fee-5bb7-950f-fb7aa1ce4e11" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Infinite Limits of Exponential Functions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115372 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6835b52b-adaa-5c66-9f6d-d32591864659" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Infinite Limits Using the Laws of Exponents and the Algebra of Limits"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116435 :tutorial/content "Consider the graph of $y=e^x$ below. We will use this graph to compute $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\, 1} e^x \\, .\n$$\n\n![](images/9d/9d83670c0d8b06b6412c8ca22a73a91ca0c2e05290eb0dbe34418cdf8e8f242c.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $1,$ the value of $y$ approaches $e^1.$ So\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1} e^x = e^1 = e.\n$$\n\nThe limit of the function at a point is equal to the value of the function at that point, and this is true for *any* point, not just $x=1.$ If $c$ is any real number, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to c} e^x = e^c.\n$$\n\nIn fact, *all* exponential functions have this property. For $a \\gt 0$ and any real number $c,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to c} a^x = a^c.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "17b24df3-bcf5-557f-b9de-7a757cca0782" :tutorial/math-academy-id 206 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116436 :tutorial/content "Consider the graphs of $y = 2^x$ and $y = 2^{-x}$ shown below. We will use these graphs to compute the limits at infinity.\n\n![](images/a3/a3189dbd6f2eb17099aff5b4fd533ce19277f6aa0021239aa2fe812ac86c7a7a.png)\n\nConsidering $y = 2^x$ first, we note that as $x \\to \\infty,$ the function increases without bound, and as $x \\to -\\infty,$ the function approaches zero. So\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty} 2^x = \\infty\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim_{x\\to -\\infty} 2^x =0.\n$$\n\nThe same properties hold true for any exponential function $y=a^x$ where $a \\gt 1.$\n\nNow consider the curve $y=2^{-x}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty} 2^{-x} = 0\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim_{x\\to -\\infty} 2^{-x} =\\infty.\n$$\n\nAgain, the same properties are true for any exponential function $y=a^{-x}$ for $a \\gt 1.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3e05ae14-8ec4-559f-b9a5-bda0ddcc5d57" :tutorial/math-academy-id 208 :tutorial/title "Infinite Limits of Exponential Functions"}]}
